ha":118285,"grapes":[["Catarratto Bianco",31112,"W","Catarratto Bianco"],["Nero d'Avola",19599,"R","Nero d'Avola"],["Grillo",9472,"W","Grillo"],["Inzolia",5665,"W","Inzolia"],["Syrah",5384,"R","Syrah"],["Chardonnay",4218,"W","Chardonnay"],["Garganega",3664,"W","Garganega"],["Merlot",3496,"R","Merlot"],["Pinot Grigio",3121,"G","Pinot Gris"],["Nerello Mascalese",2890,"R","Nerello Mascalese"],["Trebbiano Toscano",2573,"W","Trebbiano Toscano"],["Muscat of Alexandria",2284,"W","Muscat of Alexandria"],["Other",24807,"","other"]],"legal":["main","main","main","main","main","main","main","main","main","main","main","main",""]},"area_km2":25811,"rocks":{"families":[["limestone",0.71],["shale",0.14],["volcanic",0.14]],"points":14,"scale":"medium","names":{"limestone":"Limestone","shale":"Shale and mudstone","volcanic":"Volcanic rock"}},"large":true,"reading":{"climate":[]},"winkler":["IV",""],"research":{"read":"2026-11-05","sheet":{"place":"Sicilia DOC covers the whole island. It allows white, red, rosé and sparkling wines, and it names 31 grapes, from Grillo and Nero d'Avola to Chardonnay. Sparkling can be tank or classic method."},"claims":[{"topic":"history","claim":"Sicilia began as an IGT (D.M. 10 October 1995). It became a DOC by D.M. of 22 November 2011 (Gazzetta Ufficiale 284, 6 December 2011). The latest amendment listed is D.M. 27 August 2024.","strength":"Strong"},{"topic":"place","claim":"The grape zone is the whole administrative territory of the Region of Sicily. Winemaking, mandatory aging and bottling must also take place in Sicily, apart from a few firms with individual permits to bottle elsewhere.","strength":"Strong"},{"topic":"place","claim":"The disciplinare describes the island as mountainous in the north, hilly in the centre-south and south-west, a plateau in the south-east and volcanic in the east, with flat land mostly on the coasts. It lists soil groups from volcanic to clay-marl, evaporite, sand-calcarenite and limestone.","strength":"Strong"},{"topic":"grapes","claim":"The DOC allows white, red, rosé, white and rosé sparkling, and wines named for one of 31 grapes. Examples are Grillo, Catarratto, Inzolia, Carricante, Grecanico, Zibibbo, Nero d'Avola, Frappato, Nerello Mascalese, Perricone, Syrah, Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon. Chardonnay, Sauvignon, Pinot Grigio, Pinot Nero, Vermentino and Sangiovese are also on the list.","strength":"Strong"},{"topic":"grapes","claim":"White wines need at least 50% from Insolia, Catarratto, Grillo, Grecanico, Chardonnay, Viognier and Sauvignon, alone or together. Reds need at least 50% from Nero d'Avola, Frappato, Nerello Mascalese, Perricone, Syrah, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon and Nerello Cappuccio. Other grapes suited to Sicily may fill the rest.","strength":"Strong"},{"topic":"winemaking","claim":"Sparkling Sicilia must be made by natural fermentation, either by the tank (Charmat) method or by the classic method of bottle refermentation. The classic method is only for white and rosé.","strength":"Strong"},{"topic":"winemaking","claim":"Sparkling may be white or rosé, and may be named for Grillo, Chardonnay, Catarratto, Carricante, Grecanico, Pinot Grigio, Moscato Bianco, Zibibbo, Nero d'Avola, Frappato, Nerello Mascalese or Pinot Nero. Most run from brut nature to extra dry. Moscato Bianco and Zibibbo sparkling wines are sweet.","strength":"Strong"},{"topic":"alcohol","claim":"Minimum total alcohol for sparkling is 11.5% for tank-method white and rosé and 12% for classic method. Sweet Moscato Bianco and Zibibbo sparkling wines need 10.5 to 11%. Classic-method white needs at least 5.5 g/l total acidity, and classic rosé 5.0 g/l.","strength":"Strong"},{"topic":"winemaking","claim":"Late harvest (vendemmia tardiva) and passito wines are made from grapes dried on the vine, or after picking on mats, racks or crates in suitable rooms. Heat-assisted dehumidifying is banned.","strength":"Strong"},{"topic":"place","claim":"The disciplinare lists extra types: Bianco in Superiore and Riserva, Rosso in Riserva, and several grape names in Superiore or Riserva.","strength":"Strong"},{"topic":"history","claim":"The disciplinare traces Sicilian wine back through the Greeks, Romans, Arabs, Normans and Spanish. It says the Muslims brought Zibibbo to Pantelleria and names it after Capo Zebib in Africa. The more common view links the name to the Arabic zabib, raisin.","strength":"Strong"}],"sources":[{"name":"Disciplinare di produzione, Sicilia DOC (Masaf text, amended to D.M. 27.08.2024), as hosted by AIS Italia","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Sicilia/DOC/Sicilia.pdf"}],"gaps":["Consorzio di Tutela Vini DOC Sicilia not opened, so no number on its size or members.","Origin of the name Sicilia not researched.","Yield limits and still-wine alcohol minimums not extracted.","No climate or elevation figures for the island."]},"terms":[["DOC","Denominazione di Origine Controllata. The label names a precise area, and the wine is tested during production."]],"blurb":{"text":"A DOC for the whole Sicilian region. It covers still, sparkling and sweet styles."},"study":true,"levels":[3],"sources":[{"name":"Wikidata sitelinks and Wikimedia page views","url":"https://www.wikidata.org"},{"name":"Candiago et al. 2022, A geospatial inventory of regulatory information for wine protected designations of origin in Europe, Scientific Data (product specifications as of 2021)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-022-01513-0"},{"name":"Fick and Hijmans 2017, WorldClim 2.1 monthly averages for 1970 to 2000, 2.5 minute grid (about 4.5 km)","url":"https://www.worldclim.org/data/worldclim21.html"},{"name":"Beck et al. 2023, High-resolution (1 km) Koppen-Geiger maps, Scientific Data (1991 to 2020)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-023-02549-6"},{"name":"Winkler, Cook, Kliewer and Lider 1974, General Viticulture, University of California Press (heat summation regions)","url":null},{"name":"Jones 2010, Climate, grapes, and wine: structure and suitability in a variable and changing climate, VIII International Terroir Congress (which grapes suit which growing season temperatures)","url":"https://ives-openscience.eu/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/Climate_Grape_Wine_Jones.pdf"},{"name":"Anderson, Nelgen and Puga, Database of Regional, National and Global Winegrape Bearing Areas by Variety, 2000 to 2023, University of Adelaide (CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://doi.org/10.25909/32870405.v1"},{"name":"Wikipedia, Italian wine (what the label terms mean)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Italian_wine&oldid=1382972195"},{"name":"Wikipedia, Sicilia (vino) (Italian), the opening of the article","url":"https://it.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sicilia_%28vino%29&oldid=152531577"},{"name":"Eurostat GISCO, NUTS 2021 boundaries","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/web/gisco/geodata/statistical-units/territorial-units-statistics"},{"name":"GeoNames, towns of 5,000 people or more (cities5000), CC BY 4.0, for the towns on the map","url":"https://download.geonames.org/export/dump/"}],"src_for":{"climate":[2,3,4,5],"planted":[6],"map":[9]},"outline":{"w":328.36,"h":366.76,"d":"M50 88L68 76L71 69L78 81L87 87L99 81L104 69L113 70L122 65L130 78L142 77L144 85L161 93L185 85L209 89L229 85L239 81L249 71L264 68L281 75L290 69L292 60L296 66L319 56L328 60L322 63L314 81L290 116L287 136L279 146L280 164L293 173L291 181L301 199L285 210L280 224L283 235L278 239L272 233L259 230L252 233L226 224L217 205L206 194L190 188L174 190L161 184L144 169L134 168L119 157L109 146L96 145L88 137L66 138L59 129L48 122L44 111ZM262 31L267 32L272 48L266 47L261 32L254 25L260 25ZM4 218L11 222L9 229L0 222ZM36 87L39 99L37 100L30 97Z"}}